# Copy to .env and read it through once.
#
# This is a DEV deployment on a VM, not a laptop sandbox. Every credential
# below is a shipped default, which means it is public -- change all of them
# before the stack is reachable by anyone but you. The adapter keypairs are the
# exception: bin/setup.py generates those into keys/keys.json, and they are
# never written here.

# ---- who can reach it ------------------------------------------------------
# Every published port except the gateway's 80 and 443 is bound to 127.0.0.1,
# written literally in docker-compose.yml rather than taken from a variable.
# That is deliberate: one variable that moves every port to the public
# interface at once is a footgun, and the ports that should be reachable are
# reachable through the gateway instead.
#
# So from a workstation, a tunnel:
#
#   ssh -L 9202:127.0.0.1:9202 -L 8081:127.0.0.1:8081 you@the-vm
#
# Behind those loopback ports sit Keycloak's admin console and a registry whose
# write token any reader of this file can mint. Publishing one means editing
# docker-compose.yml, which is where that argument belongs.

# ---- the images ------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing is built here. `docker compose up -d` pulls these and starts them.
#
# Set both to the tags published for this environment. There is deliberately no
# working default: an unset or wrong value fails on pull, naming the tag it
# tried, which is a better failure than silently running something else.
#
# If they live in a private registry, log in on the VM first:
#   docker login ghcr.io
#
# THE ADAPTER IMAGE AND THE ADAPTER CONFIGS IN THIS REPO MOVE TOGETHER. The
# configs name plugins by id -- WeatherObservation, MandiPrice,
# KnowledgeAdvisory, AgricultureFacility -- and an id is the basename of a .so
# inside the image. Point this at an image built before one of those existed
# and every adapter dies at startup with
#
#   unrecognized step: AgricultureFacility
#
# which reads like a config typo and is not.
#
# The coupling runs the other way too, and it is why this must be set to an
# image built from the current adapter: auth is now declared PER PROVIDER, in a
# block named for the participant id. An older binary does not understand that
# nesting, and a newer binary refuses the old flat authScheme rather than
# ignoring it -- so neither half tolerates the other's config.
#
# BOTH ARE PINNED TO A VERSION, not to latest, and that is the point: what is
# deployed is written down here rather than being whatever the tag pointed at on
# the day someone last pulled. Moving version is an edit to these two lines,
# reviewable in a diff, instead of an invisible change under a moving tag.
#
# Changing a line here is not enough on its own. `pull_policy: missing` in
# docker-compose.yml means an image already on disk is never re-fetched, so
# `make up` alone can leave the old one running:
#
#   docker compose pull provider-adapter network-adapter consumer-adapter
#   docker compose up -d --force-recreate provider-adapter network-adapter consumer-adapter
#
# Both halves matter: the pull without the recreate leaves the old container
# running, and the recreate without the pull recreates it on the old image.
ADAPTER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/openagrinet/network-adapter:v0.0.1-RC1
DISCOVERY_IMAGE=ghcr.io/openagrinet/discovery-service:v0.0.1-RC1

# KNOWN STALE as of 2026-09-10, and stale in the way that does not announce
# itself. That tag was built before the telemetry package existed -- read its
# embedded build info and there is no go.opentelemetry.io dependency at all:
#
#   docker run --rm ghcr.io/nisargabd/discovery-service:latest --version
#   go version -m <the binary>        # no go.opentelemetry.io/* lines
#
# Against it, every OTEL_* setting under ---- discovery ---- below is accepted
# and none of it does anything. Traces never arrive, no error says so, and the
# stack reads as healthy throughout. Nothing here can detect that for you.
#
# To run what is actually in the discovery-service repo instead:
#
#   DISCOVERY_IMAGE=discovery-service:local docker compose build discovery
#   DISCOVERY_IMAGE=discovery-service:local docker compose up -d discovery
#
# or set DISCOVERY_IMAGE=discovery-service:local here and drop the prefixes.
# discovery is the ONLY service with a build: section; see the comment on it.
#
# Where that build reads its source from. The default assumes the two repos are
# siblings, which is how they sit for anyone who has both. An absolute path is
# fine. Nothing consults this unless you run `docker compose build discovery`.
DISCOVERY_CONTEXT=../../discovery-service

# The build's base images, already defaulted to public ones in the compose file
# because the Dockerfile's own defaults are dhi.io/* and those 401 without a
# Docker Hardened Images subscription. Set these only if you have that
# subscription and want the same bases CI uses:
#
#   DISCOVERY_BUILD_IMAGE=dhi.io/golang:1.27-alpine-dev
#   DISCOVERY_RUNTIME_IMAGE=dhi.io/static:20260611-musl-alpine

# The two mock upstreams. Their sources are in mock-server/, to be built and
# published once rather than built here -- see mock-server/README.md for the build
# commands and for what they deliberately get wrong.
MOCKIMD_IMAGE=ghcr.io/nisargabd/oan-mockimd:latest
MOCKAGMARKNET_IMAGE=ghcr.io/nisargabd/oan-mockagmarknet:latest

# ---- ports -----------------------------------------------------------------
REGISTRY_PORT=8081
KEYCLOAK_PORT=8080
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PORT=9990
DISCOVERY_PORT=8090
PROVIDER_ADAPTER_PORT=9200
NETWORK_ADAPTER_PORT=9201
EXP_ADAPTER_PORT=9202
# The mocks are published on loopback for looking at them directly while
# debugging. The adapter reaches them by compose service name, not through
# these.
MOCKIMD_PORT=9100
MOCKAGMARKNET_PORT=9101

# ---- registry --------------------------------------------------------------
# CHANGE every credential in this block before the stack is exposed.
REGISTRY_VERSION=v2.0.0
POSTGRES_DB=registry
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
KEYCLOAK_REALM=sunbird-rc
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin
KEYCLOAK_SECRET=1b46c0f2-6a1c-4a2f-8b0f-2b6b30bcd2b2
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=admin-api
KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=registry-frontend
REGISTRY_DEFAULT_USER_PASSWORD=abcd@123
REGISTRY_USER=no-user
REGISTRY_PASSWORD=no-user-password

# ---- telemetry -------------------------------------------------------------
# The three adapters ship metrics, traces and logs over OTLP/gRPC to the
# collector named here. hyperdx is ClickStack, on the observability profile,
# which `make up` starts and `make up-core` does not.
#
# ONE SWITCH, THREE SIGNALS. With OTEL_ENABLED=false the plugin builds no
# exporter and never dials -- so it cannot log a refused connection every few
# seconds. Set it false for `make up-core`, or for any stack whose collector
# is not running; leaving it true against a missing collector is the noisy
# case, not a broken one.
#
# Changing any of these means re-running bin/setup.py and recreating the
# adapters: they are rendered into the configs, not read at runtime.
OTEL_ENABLED=true
OTLP_ENDPOINT=hyperdx:4317
OTEL_ENVIRONMENT=dev

# ---- discovery -------------------------------------------------------------
APP_NETWORK_ID=oan-dev
BECKN_SPEC_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beckn/protocol-specifications-v2/refs/tags/core-v2.0.0-lts/api/v2.0.0/beckn.yaml

# Discovery's telemetry. It reaches hyperdx through OTLP_ENDPOINT above, the
# same way the adapters do.
#
# `otlp` because `make up` starts the observability profile. FOR `make up-core`,
# WHICH DOES NOT, SET THIS TO none: the exporter does not dial at startup and no
# request ever waits on telemetry, so a missing hyperdx costs a periodic export
# failure in the log rather than anything worse -- but it is noise, and this is
# the switch that silences it. Same trade as OTEL_ENABLED above.
#
# What you will NOT see in HyperDX is the five OAN metric.code streams. They are
# derived from spans by a collector this stack does not run -- see Appendix H.
OTEL_EXPORTER=otlp

# REQUIRED whenever OTEL_EXPORTER is otlp -- discovery refuses to boot without
# it rather than exporting a stream that cannot be attributed. It is the
# Resource's `producer` and every span's recipient.id, so an empty value files
# the whole stream under no participant, which reads as data rather than as an
# absence.
#
# Hostname-shaped and environment-specific, like the three adapter identities
# below and for the same reason: a dev stream must not be mistakable for a real
# one on a shared network. Unlike them it needs no keypair and no registry row --
# discovery does not sign, so setup.py does not register it.
DISCOVERY_SUBSCRIBER_ID=discovery.oan.dev

# The sector, and the Resource's `domain`. Checked against the sector list the
# build declares, so a typo fails the boot instead of quietly splitting this
# deployment's telemetry into a group of one. Every OAN component must emit the
# identical string.
DISCOVERY_DOMAIN=Agriculture

# ---- the three adapter identities ------------------------------------------
# bin/setup.py registers exactly these three in the registry and generates a
# keypair for each.
#
# A participant id IS the network identity -- the id a signature is verified
# against -- so the registry requires it to be hostname-shaped.
# They are never resolved by DNS: routing between the adapters is the router
# plugin's config, which uses the compose service names.
#
# Pick these once and deliberately. The registry is append-only: there is no
# update, delete is soft, and a soft-deleted id keeps the unique index -- so an
# id can never be reused. Name them for the environment they are, so a dev
# identity cannot be mistaken for a real one on a shared network.
EXP_SUBSCRIBER_ID=exp.oan.dev
NETWORK_SUBSCRIBER_ID=network.oan.dev
PROVIDER_SUBSCRIBER_ID=provider.oan.dev

# ---- the four upstream providers -------------------------------------------
# bin/setup.py creates ALL of these in the registry, along with their
# capability bindings -- seven participants and four bindings in all. It has to:
# the registry is not reachable from outside this stack, so there is no second
# way to create them.
#
# WHY THESE MUST MATCH THE ROWS. The provider adapter decides whether a request
# is its own by building a binding key out of the incoming payload -- the
# provider id and the capability @type it carries -- and comparing it against
# the keys in its own config. setup.py renders these values into that config
# AND seeds the registry from them, which is what keeps the two from
# disagreeing. Change one here and re-run setup.py; changing only one side is
# how the failures below happen.
#
# A disagreement fails, and how depends on which side is wrong:
#
#   the payload names a provider no step is configured for
#     404 NET_ENTITY_NOT_FOUND, "this module serves no capability matching the
#     request". Each step passes through what is not its own -- which is what
#     lets one adapter host all four capabilities -- and nothing behind them
#     answers.
#
#   a step IS configured for the key but the registry has no matching
#   ProviderSchema row
#     404 too, now naming the binding with no active record. Before that it was
#     a 500 with the reason only in the log.
#
# The base URLs are compose service names: both upstreams are mocks called from
# inside this network and reached from nowhere else. Pointing a capability at a
# real API means a new Participant and ProviderSchema row, written from inside
# the stack, and the id here updated to match.
PROVIDER_PARTICIPANT_ID=mausamgram-mock
PROVIDER_CAPABILITY=openagrinet:WeatherObservation
MAUSAMGRAM_BASE_URL=http://mockimd:9100
MAUSAMGRAM_PATH=/get-daily

# MandiPrice NOW TALKS TO THE REAL AGMARKNET, not the mock, because it uses
# authScheme tokenQuery and mockagmarknet has no token endpoint. Both values
# below are hosts you must fill in, and the stack needs egress to them.
#
# THE PARTICIPANT ID MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. The registry is append-only:
# there is no update, delete is soft and keeps the unique index, so an id can
# never be reused. A row already seeded against the mock KEEPS the mock's
# baseUrl -- re-running setup.py prints "already present" and changes nothing.
# So switching an existing deployment from the mock to the real API means a NEW
# MANDI_PARTICIPANT_ID here, not just a new base URL. The id below says
# "agmarknet" rather than "agmarknet-mock" for exactly that reason.
#
# Agmarknet issues its own short-lived tokens: the adapter POSTs the access
# name and password as JSON to MANDI_TOKEN_URL and sends the token it gets
# back as a query parameter. Those two are credentials and reach the adapter as
# environment variables only -- never in a config file or a registry row.
MANDI_PARTICIPANT_ID=agmarknet
MANDI_CAPABILITY=openagrinet:MandiPrice
MANDI_BASE_URL=<the Agmarknet base URL>
MANDI_PATH=/v1/fetch-agmarknet-vistaar
MANDI_TOKEN_URL=<the Agmarknet token endpoint>
MANDI_ACCESS_NAME=<the Agmarknet access name>
MANDI_PASSWORD=<the Agmarknet password>

# KNOWN AND UNFIXED, before you point this at the real API. Agmarknet answers
# "no records for that market and date" with HTTP 400, not with an empty list.
# The adapter treats a 4xx as a permanent failure, so a farmer whose market has
# no prices today gets an ERROR rather than "no prices found". Measured against
# the live API, and it is a code fix, not a config one.

# The knowledge capability, and the one exception to the paragraph above: this
# is a REAL external provider, not a mock in this network. So its base URL is a
# host rather than a compose service name, and the stack needs egress to it.
# Its action is a POST, unlike the two GETs above.
#
# It authenticates with OAuth2 client credentials. The two values below are the
# only secrets in this file; the adapter reads them from its environment and
# they appear in no config file and in no registry row. Fill them in from your
# own service account -- they are deliberately left as placeholders, because a
# credential committed to an example file is a credential to rotate.
KNOWLEDGE_PARTICIPANT_ID=knowledge-provider
KNOWLEDGE_CAPABILITY=openagrinet:KnowledgeAdvisory
KNOWLEDGE_BASE_URL=<the provider base URL>
KNOWLEDGE_PATH=/docs-pipeline-api/search
KNOWLEDGE_TOKEN_URL=<the provider OAuth2 token endpoint>
KNOWLEDGE_CLIENT_ID=<the service account client id>
KNOWLEDGE_CLIENT_SECRET=<the service account secret>

# POCRA, serving AgricultureFacility. A real external provider as well, and the
# only capability that needs NO credential -- so there is nothing secret here,
# and its registry row publishes authScheme none.
#
# Its action is a POST, like knowledge. The base URL below is POCRA's published
# host; override it if you have been given a different one.
#
# TWO MEASURED BEHAVIOURS WORTH KNOWING before you point traffic at it.
#
# One: it takes exactly ONE facility type per call. A comma-separated pair
# answers 200 with no providers and an array is refused, so a payload asking
# for three types becomes three calls that the adapter merges. Each call needs
# its OWN message_id -- POCRA caches on that id for ten minutes and IGNORES
# location, so a reused id returns facilities from wherever it was first asked.
# The mapping derives one per call; nothing here to configure.
#
# Two: after roughly twenty calls in a few minutes it starts answering 200 with
# an EMPTY catalogue, recovering after a pause. A healthy "nothing nearby" and
# a rate-limited answer look identical, so the adapter cannot tell them apart.
# That is why searchConcurrency is pinned to 1 in the provider config. Do not
# raise it.
POCRA_PARTICIPANT_ID=pocra
POCRA_CAPABILITY=openagrinet:AgricultureFacility
POCRA_BASE_URL=https://middleware-bap-client.mahapocra.gov.in
POCRA_PATH=/search

# THE MOCK'S token, and only the mock's, now that MandiPrice authenticates
# against the real API with tokenQuery. mockagmarknet answers 401 without it.
#
# Still here because it is what you set when pointing MandiPrice back at the
# mock: authScheme query, queryName token, queryValueEnv MANDI_TOKEN. Left
# unread by the adapter in the default configuration above.
MANDI_TOKEN=local-mandi-token

# How many records each mock answers with. The mappings read however many
# arrive, so these are the knobs for checking that they do. mockagmarknet's
# last record reports no minimum or maximum, as the real data sometimes does.
MOCKIMD_DAYS=3
MOCKAGMARKNET_DAYS=2

# The mappings each provider adapter fetches, request and response in one file
# per binding-action. The registry row holds the full URL and the adapter
# fetches it verbatim.
#
# This repo's own copies, served over the raw CDN -- the same files that sit in
# config/mappings/ beside these configs, so what the adapter fetches and what a
# reader reviews are one file and cannot drift.
#
# Note the branch in the paths. These now name development rather than the
# feature branch they were pinned to, which is where the refreshed mappings
# land -- so they resolve once this merges, not before. Pin a tag instead if a
# deployment should not follow a moving file.
#
# To change a mapping: edit config/mappings/, push, and the next cache expiry
# picks it up -- about a minute for the adapter plus a few for GitHub's CDN.
MAPPING_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenAgriNet/helmcharts/release-0.0.1/quick-start/config/mappings/mausamgram/weather-observation.select.yaml
MANDI_MAPPING_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenAgriNet/helmcharts/release-0.0.1/quick-start/config/mappings/agmarknet/mandi-price.select.yaml
KNOWLEDGE_MAPPING_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenAgriNet/helmcharts/release-0.0.1/quick-start/config/mappings/knowledge/knowledge-advisory.select.yaml
POCRA_MAPPING_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenAgriNet/helmcharts/release-0.0.1/quick-start/config/mappings/pocra/agriculture-facility.select.yaml
